    "title": "1. Where I Stand: Social Location Map (20%)",
    "name": "Where I Stand: Social Location Map",
    "summary": "Create a visual map of the institutions, roles, places, relationships, systems, and social conditions that shape how you encounter society. Explain two routes through the map and how your place in society affects what you notice or may overlook.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 20% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 20 points.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">SOC122, Fall 2026. Weight: 20 percent.</p><h3>Your task</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Create a visual map of the institutions, roles, places, relationships, systems, and social conditions that shape how you encounter society. Explain two routes through the map and how your place in society affects what you notice or may overlook.</p><h3>What to submit</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One readable visual map OR a clearly labelled text map showing the connections.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A 500 to 700 word explanation.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One source of access or advantage and one barrier or limit.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One course concept and one question your position makes easier or harder to see.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A short privacy note naming what you chose not to include.</li></ul><h3>Boundary</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">You control the level of disclosure. Identity proof, trauma, private family history, and legal or financial details are not required.</p><h3>Keep the scope manageable</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Focus on four or five influences rather than your whole life. A role such as student, worker or neighbour is enough. Social location means how your roles and circumstances shape your opportunities and viewpoint. Structural analysis means explaining how an institution or social condition shapes an experience, not only describing individual choices.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">You may use a low-disclosure role-based example. Explain two connected routes, one source of access and one barrier, using one taught concept. No outside research or disclosure of a protected identity is required.</p><h3>How your work will be graded</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Strong means 85 to 100 percent of a criterion, Clear means 70 to 84 percent, Developing means 50 to 69 percent, and Limited means 0 to 49 percent.</p><h3>Map evidence: 5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: Institutions, roles, places, relationships, and forces form a readable system.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: The main map elements and pathways are clear.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: The map is partial or weakly connected.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: The map cannot be used to understand your position.</li></ul><h3>Structural analysis: 6 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: Access, advantage, constraint, and visibility are analysed without reducing them to individual choice.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: The main structural effects are clear.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: Analysis is mostly descriptive or individualised.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: Structural analysis is absent or unsupported.</li></ul><h3>Course concept and question: 5 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: A concept sharpens a thoughtful question about what the position reveals or hides.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: The concept and question are relevant and clear.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: The connection is thin or generic.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: The concept or question is absent or substantially inaccurate.</li></ul><h3>Communication and privacy: 4 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Strong: The visual or text map and explanation are clear, accessible, and respect privacy.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Clear: The work is clear and respects privacy.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Developing: Clarity or privacy choices are uneven.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limited: The work is difficult to follow or requires inappropriate disclosure.</li></ul><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Your grade is based on the evidence in your work. Personal disclosure, polished production, and technical effects do not replace course learning.</p><h3>How to submit in Blackboard</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assignment and select Start attempt. Write or paste your written work into the Submission editor using the headings in these instructions. A Word upload is optional.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">If you choose a visual format, insert it with a text description or attach it as an accessible file. If you choose an audio or video format, attach the recording and captions or a transcript. A working Seneca OneDrive or Microsoft Stream link is also acceptable.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Keep all required parts together in this assignment. Before submitting, check that your text, attachments, and links open correctly.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select Submit, wait for Blackboard’s confirmation, and keep the receipt. If you save a draft instead, return to submit it before the deadline.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Deadlines and submission window</h3><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">Released: September 18, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due: September 30, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</strong></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">The 48-hour no-penalty submission window ends October 2, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern. Routine extension requests must arrive before the posted due date, not during this window.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">If Blackboard labels work submitted within the allowed window as late, that label does not mean a penalty applies. Work after the applicable window receives zero unless an extension or exception is approved. Unexpected emergencies and approved accommodations are considered separately.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><a href=\"deadline-policy.html\">Read the full Deadlines and Extensions policy</a>.</p>",
