    "title": "2. Two-Eyed Seeing Observation Journal: Set 1 (10%)",
    "name": "Two-Eyed Seeing Observation Journal: Set 1",
    "summary": "Complete two dated entries. Begin with a specific item from class, public issue, or low-risk observation. Explain what one social science approach reveals. Then use a specific Indigenous or Two-Eyed Seeing course source to show what changes, expands, or remains outside that account.",
    "html": "<p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 10% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 10 points.</p><h3>Purpose</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Complete two dated entries. Begin with a specific item from class, public issue, or low-risk observation. Explain what one social science approach reveals. Then use a specific Indigenous or Two-Eyed Seeing course source to show what changes, expands, or remains outside that account.</p><h3>Each entry must include</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A date and specific observation.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">The social science discipline or method used first.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">A named Indigenous or Two-Eyed Seeing course source.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">What becomes visible, changes, or remains unresolved when the sources are held together.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">One limit, question, or next step.</li></ul><h3>Boundary</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Do not invent an Indigenous perspective, treat different Indigenous Nations as if they have one shared view, or speak for a community. Use the assigned author or organization accurately.</p><h3>Length and preparation</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Aim for 150 to 200 words per entry, or a one-to-two-minute audio entry with a transcript. Use the five prompts as a guide, not five separate essays. Choose assigned sources; you do not need to find new scholarship.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use material from Weeks 2 to 5. Explain one social-science idea or method and one idea from a named Indigenous or Two-Eyed Seeing course source. You are comparing what the specific sources help you notice, not claiming expertise in a whole knowledge system.</p><h3>How Set 1 is graded</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Dated observation evidence: 2.5 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">source accuracy and respect: 3 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">analysis using both sources of knowledge: 3 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">communication and limits: 1.5 points.</li></ul><h3>How to post your journal entries</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this journal in Blackboard and find Entries. Start a separate entry for each required date or example.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Write directly in the journal editor. Put the entry number and date at the top. You do not need to upload a Word document.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">If you choose an audio entry, attach the recording and a transcript, or add a Seneca-accessible recording link.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Select Post, then reopen the entry to confirm it is there. You may edit your entries through the submission window stated below. Only you and the teaching team can read this journal.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own observations, choices, explanations, and sources. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your submission, invent experiences or evidence, or create narration in your place. You may use spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and accessibility tools that do not generate the course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Privacy and accessibility</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">You control what personal information you share. Remove identifying details about other people. Use readable headings and descriptive links. Add a text description for meaningful visuals, and captions or a transcript for audio or video. Your grade reflects your learning and reasoning; private disclosure and production effects do not earn extra marks.</p><h3>Deadlines and submission window</h3><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">Released: September 18, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due: October 14, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</strong></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">The 48-hour no-penalty submission window ends October 16, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern. Routine extension requests must arrive before the posted due date, not during this window.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">If Blackboard labels work submitted within the allowed window as late, that label does not mean a penalty applies. Work after the applicable window receives zero unless an extension or exception is approved. Unexpected emergencies and approved accommodations are considered separately.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><a href=\"deadline-policy.html\">Read the full Deadlines and Extensions policy</a>.</p>",
