    "id": "research-choices",
    "title": "3. Research Choices Lab (20%)",
    "name": "Research Choices Lab",
    "summary": "How do research choices affect what we can learn and whose experiences we include? Use ideas from the research methods and ethics work you have studied so far. You are exploring a study design, not expected to be an expert researcher.",
    "html": "<h3>Research Choices Lab</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Worth 20% of your final course grade. Maximum score: 20 points.</p><h3>What you will explore</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">How do research choices affect what we can learn and whose experiences we include? Use ideas from the research methods and ethics work you have studied so far. You are exploring a study design, not expected to be an expert researcher.</p><h3>Watch first</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\"><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZFJUYS6wY7U\">CBC Marketplace: Why you’re addicted to your smartphone</a></p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use the investigation as a starting point for questions about smartphones and digital life. A documentary is a source to examine, not proof that every claim in it is correct. The three scenarios below are fictional research situations inspired by the topic; their details are not reported results from the video.</p><h3>Part A: make and explain three research choices</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">For each scenario, select one option using the numbered control. The choice earns 0 points. There is no answer key: different choices can be defended. Your written explanation earns up to 2 points.</p><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">For each explanation, write 3 to 5 focused sentences: identify your choice, explain why you would start there, and name one benefit and one limitation or risk. Connect your reasoning to a course idea. In at least one explanation, refer to a specific moment from the video, using a rough timestamp or a clear description.</p><h3>Part B: design a small study</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Answer Questions 7 to 9 about one manageable study on phones, apps, screen habits, or digital connection. A short paragraph or clear bullet points for each answer is enough. There is no total word requirement. Design the study only: do not recruit people, collect private data, or carry it out.</p><h3>Build on earlier feedback</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Before you start, revisit the feedback on Assessment 1. Choose one suggestion that can help your reasoning or use of evidence here. You do not need a grade or feedback on an assignment that is still being marked.</p><h3>How your work is graded: 20 points</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Three scenario explanations: 2 points each, 6 points total.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Your study question and plan: 6 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Consent, privacy, and accountability: 5 points.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Limits and communication: 3 points.</li></ul><h3>How to submit</h3><ul style=\"margin:0;padding-left:28px;\"><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Open this assessment in Blackboard and select Start attempt.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Write directly in each response editor. You do not need to upload a Word document.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">You may submit up to three attempts. Your latest submitted attempt is the one I will grade. Include all required answers in that attempt.</li><li style=\"margin:0 0 8px;line-height:1.6;\">Before the deadline, review your answers, select Submit, and wait for the confirmation. Keep the receipt. Save and Close saves your work but does not submit it.</li></ul><h3>Your work and permitted tools</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">Use your own choices, observations, and explanations. You may return to the assigned video and course materials. Do not use generative AI to write or rewrite your answers or invent evidence. Spelling, dictation, screen-reader, captioning, and other accessibility tools are welcome when they do not generate your course content. Ask me before using a tool if you are unsure.</p><h3>Privacy and access</h3><p style=\"margin:0;line-height:1.6;\">You do not need to share personal health information, private messages, device records, or another person’s story. Use fictional or public examples when needed. Your grade is based on your reasoning and course learning, not private disclosure or polished production. If a video, caption, or response editor is difficult to access, contact me through Faculty Contact Information before the deadline so we can arrange an accessible way to complete the task.</p><h3>Deadlines and submission window</h3><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">Released: October 13, 2026 at 12:01 a.m. Eastern.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><strong>Due: October 20, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern.</strong></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">The 48-hour no-penalty submission window ends October 22, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. Eastern. Routine extension requests must arrive before the posted due date, not during this window.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\">If Blackboard labels work submitted within the allowed window as late, that label does not mean a penalty applies. Work after the applicable window receives zero unless an extension or exception is approved. Unexpected emergencies and approved accommodations are considered separately.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 16px;line-height:1.6;\"><a href=\"deadline-policy.html\">Read the full Deadlines and Extensions policy</a>.</p>",
